❯ Think briefly, then run ls here, then reply with 4 bullets of about 30 words each on how terminal
  emulators measure character width.

  Listed 1 directory

⏺ The directory is empty: ls returned no output.

  - Cell grid: Terminals draw text on a fixed grid, so each character occupies one or more cells.
    Emulators decide that count per code point, usually from Unicode data, and the app must agree or
    columns drift.
  - East Asian Width: Characters marked Wide or Fullwidth take two cells, and most CJK text and many
    emoji fall in this group. Combining marks and zero-width characters take zero cells.
  - Ambiguous width: Characters marked Ambiguous, such as some Greek, Cyrillic and box-drawing
    symbols, render as one or two cells depending on locale or a user setting, so the same text can
    misalign between terminals.
  - Grapheme clusters and emoji: Emoji sequences with ZWJ joiners, skin tones or variation selectors
    are inconsistent across emulators. Newer terminals measure whole grapheme clusters, while older
    ones count each code point separately.

